hw/sim/ - GHDL testbenches
==========================

Self-checking VHDL-2008 testbenches. Each asserts expected values and reports
via std.env; a failed assertion fails `make -f hw.mk sim`.

    tb_pe.vhd         drives one pe through every ALU opcode and operand
                      select, checking the register after each step.
    tb_uart.vhd       loops uart_tx.tx_serial back into uart_rx and checks that
                      adversarial byte patterns survive framing + mid-bit
                      sampling bit-exact. Has a watchdog that fails on a stall.
    tb_cgra_top.vhd   full-chip: feeds real UART byte streams into cgra_top
                      and checks CFG/WR/RUN/RD/RST, checksums and NACKs at
                      the wire level. This is the protocol conformance test.
    tb_matvec.vhd     exercises the weight-stationary systolic matrix-vector
                      mapping end to end.

All are self-checking (assert ... severity failure + std.env.finish), so a
failure fails `make sim`; there is nothing to eyeball in a waveform.

Run
---
    make -f hw.mk sim     # analyse, elaborate and run all four (assert-level=error)
    make -f hw.mk wave    # tb_cgra_top -> build/tb_cgra_top.ghw (gtkwave)

The host-side emulator (lib/src/emu.c) is the golden reference for the same
semantics: if a testbench and the emulator disagree, one of them is wrong.
